From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: count what was written, not what was intended
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

Three TLV builders write a count and then skip entries under it. The
controller reads the count, finds fewer entries than it was promised, and
parses the rest of the TLV out of whatever follows. Nothing logs, on either
side: the sender wrote a well-formed buffer and the receiver read a
well-formed field.

  agent_gen_bss_config_report_tlv   (*num_bss)++ and the BSSID both go out,
                                    then `if (!ap->cfg) continue` leaves -
                                    so the entry is counted, six bytes are
                                    written, and the report byte and SSID
                                    are not. ap->cfg == NULL is an ORDINARY
                                    state here: map-agent deletes its own ap
                                    sections (99999991), so on a live mesh
                                    this fires rather than not.

  agent_gen_ap_mld_config           numAffiliatedAPs written before the
                                    loop; an AP that is gone or no longer
                                    affiliated is skipped and still counted.

  agent_gen_associated_sta_mld_config
                                    numAffiliatedSTAs written before the
                                    loop; a leg whose struct sta is gone is
                                    skipped and still counted. This is the
                                    mechanical source of legs that exist on
                                    the controller and nowhere else - the
                                    ghosts we had been reaping in the
                                    controller instead of preventing here.

The correct idiom is already in this file, sixty lines below the last of
them: agent_gen_bss_advanced_config() remembers the offset of the count,
writes a zero, and increments it after each entry is written. All three are
moved to it; the bss_config_report case additionally has its guard moved
above the write, because there the count was not the only thing that had
already happened.

Two more sites were checked and left alone: agent_gen_wifi7_agent_caps
counts correctly already (its `continue` is in a nested loop that writes
before it counts), and agent_gen_bsta_mld_config has no skip at all.

Verify on hardware: on a node whose ap sections have been deleted at least
once, the controller's affiliated_ap / affiliated_sta row counts must match
what the agent reports over ubus, and must stay matched across a client
roam.
--- a/src/agent_tlv.c
+++ b/src/agent_tlv.c
@@ -4012,6 +4012,20 @@
 			if (bss->enabled) {
 				int len;
 
+				/* An ap with no cfg used to be counted and then
+				 * half written: the BSSID went out, the report
+				 * byte and the SSID did not, and num_bss claimed
+				 * one more than the buffer held. The controller
+				 * read a truncated entry and everything after it
+				 * shifted under it.
+				 *
+				 * ap->cfg == NULL is an ORDINARY state here -
+				 * map-agent deletes ap sections itself - so
+				 * leave before writing anything, not after.
+				 */
+				if (!ap->cfg)
+					continue;
+
 				(*num_bss)++;
 
 				len = strlen(bss->ssid);
@@ -4022,8 +4036,6 @@
 				t->len += 6;
 
 				/*report bits */
-				if (!ap->cfg)
-					continue;
 
 				/* Here the in use flag is 0 and not in use flag is 1*
 				 * So if it is backhaul the fronthaul flag is 1
@@ -4666,6 +4678,7 @@
 
 	struct tlv_ap_mld_config *data;
 	struct netif_mld *mld = NULL;
+	int num_aff_ap_offset;
 	int offset = 0;
 	struct tlv *t;
 	int ret = 0;
@@ -4717,8 +4730,17 @@
 
 		offset += 20; /* reserved */
 
-		/* numAffiliatedAPs */
-		t->data[offset++] = mld->num_affiliated_ap;
+		/* numAffiliatedAPs
+		 *
+		 * Counted after the entry is written, not before it is tried.
+		 * An affiliated AP that is gone, or no longer affiliated, is
+		 * skipped below and the old code still claimed it - so the
+		 * controller read one entry more than the buffer held and the
+		 * rest of the TLV shifted. Same idiom as the num_radio_offset
+		 * in agent_gen_bss_advanced_config().
+		 */
+		num_aff_ap_offset = offset;
+		t->data[offset++] = 0;
 		for (i = 0; i < mld->num_affiliated_ap; i++) {
 			struct wifi_radio_element *re = NULL;
 			struct netif_ap *ap;
@@ -4727,6 +4749,8 @@
 			if (!ap || !ap->is_affiliated_ap)
 				continue;
 
+			t->data[num_aff_ap_offset]++;
+
 			/* Affiliated AP MAC Addr Valid */
 			t->data[offset] = AP_MLD_CONF_AFF_AP_ADDR_VALID;
 			// TODO: ap->link_id_valid
@@ -5123,7 +5147,13 @@
 
 	offset += 18; /* reserved */
 
-	data->num_affiliated_sta = mld->num_affiliated_sta;
+	/* Count what was written, not what was intended. A leg whose struct sta
+	 * is gone is skipped below, and the old code still claimed it: the
+	 * controller then read one entry more than the buffer held. That is the
+	 * mechanical source of legs that exist on the controller and nowhere
+	 * else - the ghosts we had been reaping instead of preventing.
+	 */
+	data->num_affiliated_sta = 0;
 	offset += 1;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < mld->num_affiliated_sta; i++) {
@@ -5133,6 +5163,8 @@
 		if (!s)
 			continue;
 
+		data->num_affiliated_sta++;
+
 		memcpy(&t->data[offset], s->bssid, 6);
 		offset += 6;
 		memcpy(&t->data[offset], s->macaddr, 6);
